# Hypothesis

**The gardening pathway does not have one prevalence style in this region; it switches style at the
boundary between *urbanised/cultivated* ground and *low-elevation, moist, disturbed (azonal)* ground —
wetlands, coastal and riparian plots. That switch is a switch in **dominance**, not in **frequency**,
and it is the reason any single prevalence metric gives a contradictory answer.**

Garden-introduced species are encountered just as often in the city as in the wetlands — the share of
plots containing at least one gardening-introduced species is 0.48 (31/65) in Urban habitat and 0.48
(32/67) in Wetlands+Coastal (OR 1.00, Fisher p = 1.0) — yet among the non-native species actually
present in those plots, gardening supplies 55% of records in Wetlands (29/52) and 54% in Coastal
(23/43) but only 30% in Urban (45/151) and 19% in Croplands (20/108), against a regional average of
35% (169/481). Formalising the two axes, Wetlands and Coastal are **frequent-and-co-dominant**
(binomial tests vs leave-one-out share: Holm p = 0.005 and 0.061; gardening outnumbered by
unintentional records in neither, both p > 0.77), whereas Urban and Croplands are
**frequent-but-subordinate** — gardening is outnumbered by unintentional (seed-contaminant, trade)
records 0.43:1 and 0.24:1 (p = 7e-07 and 1e-10). This style difference is not an artefact of urban
plots simply holding more invaders: invaded plots hold comparable total numbers of non-natives across
habitat groups (Kruskal-Wallis on plot richness p = 0.089), yet their pathway composition differs
(sharp group effects on n.gard p = 5e-06 and n.unint p = 8e-05), and it survives every context control
we applied: the azonal-vs-urbanised gardening:other occurrence odds ratio is 2.50 [1.67, 3.72]
(p = 8e-06), 2.90 [2.06, 4.09] after conditioning on 50 m urban cover, elevation and distance to road
in a quasi-binomial model, and 2.47–2.81 (all p < 1e-05) inside Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel strata defined
by urban cover, by elevation, and by plot invader richness. Adding historical (1956) cropland cover
leaves it unchanged (2.88 [2.04, 4.08]).

**The habitat boundary is itself conditional on the urbanisation gradient, and this is the sharpest
part of the answer.** Stratifying invaded plots by 1000 m urban cover, gardening's dominance advantage
in azonal over urbanised/cultivated plots is very large where urban cover is scarce (share 0.59 vs
0.09, OR 14.4 [2.68, 77.8], p = 0.001) and still strong at intermediate cover (0.65 vs 0.23, OR 6.18
[2.74, 13.9], p = 7e-06), but it collapses to nothing once urban cover exceeds 20% (0.36 vs 0.28,
OR 1.43 [0.86, 2.36], p = 0.20). The same monotonic collapse occurs as plot invader richness rises
(OR 7.35 -> 4.07 -> 1.81 for 1, 2, >=3 invaders), i.e. once a plot is saturated by introductions the
unintentional pathway swamps the gardening signal and habitat identity stops mattering. That is what
the significant CMH heterogeneity test across urban-cover strata (p = 7.8e-04) is reporting. So the
context in which gardening shows *different* prevalence styles across habitat types is specifically
**low-urban-cover, low-elevation terrain**: there, moist disturbed Wetlands and Coastal plots let garden
escapes hold >50% of the invader pool (0.59 azonal share vs 0.09 in low-cover urbanised/cultivated
plots), while Riparian is intermediate (0.365, i.e. regional-average rather than dominant) and
low-cover cropland/urban-type plots do not; inside the urbanised matrix both habitat types converge on the same subordinate style.

**Named context variables.** (1) *Urbanisation gradient* — measured as `urban.2009.1000m` and
`urban.2009.50m`: it drives the frequency axis (occupancy rises 0.025 -> 0.120 -> 0.375 -> 0.420
across 1000 m urban-cover classes; Spearman rho = 0.375) but leaves the dominance axis essentially
flat (share 0.31–0.37 in every class). Urbanisation therefore controls *how often* garden escapes are
found, not *what share* of the invader pool they hold. (2) *Elevation* — the strongest single
covariate on the frequency axis (Firth OR 0.27 [0.19, 0.40] per SD; occupancy 0.360 -> 0.167 -> 0.028
across tertiles) and the reason all dry upland habitats (Rock outcrops, Scrublands, both forest types,
Meadows; mean 511–1078 m) look gardening-free. (3) *Water/disturbance* — distance to stream itself is
not predictive (rho = 0.031, p = 0.43); what matters is membership of the moist, disturbed azonal
habitat complex (Wetlands, Coastal and, less strongly, Riparian), which stays gardening-rich -
co-dominant in Wetlands and Coastal, at least regional-average in Riparian - even after removing
urban-cover, elevation, road-proximity and plot-richness effects. Habitat identity survives all of
these: adding habitat to a context-only Firth model gives LR chi2 = 51.5, df = 2, p = 7e-12, and
adding context to a habitat model still gives p = 3e-08, so habitat and context are both informative
rather than one reducing to the other.

**Metric dependence is part of the answer.** Ranking habitats by plot-level frequency and by
occurrence-weighted dominance gives statistically unrelated orderings (Spearman rho = 0.079, p = 0.82)
across the ten habitats; once plots are restricted to invaded plots the two agree (rho = 0.985),
showing the disagreement is generated by the *proportion of uninvaded plots* per habitat, not by noise.
Reading only frequency would put Urban and Wetlands in the same class; reading only share would put
Broad-leaved forests first (share = 1.00) — but that value rests on 3 invaded plots and 4 records and
is an artefact of a vanishing denominator, so we label it undetermined.

**Honest bounds.** The natural/semi-natural upland habitats supply almost no information: Rock outcrops
contributes 1 invaded plot and 1 record (occupancy 0.000, 95% CI 0.000–0.063), Scrublands 3 invaded
plots and 5 records, so these are **no evidence of gardening / undetermined**, not demonstrated
absence. Broad-leaved (3 invaded plots, 4 records) and Coniferous forests (5, 8) and Meadows (5, 13)
are the same situation with weakly more data: their high gardening share should be read as
"garden-origin relics recorded where little else can establish", a low-n caution rather than a style.
All models are exploratory: the unpenalised habitat-level logistic is quasi-separated (singular
Hessian), so Firth penalisation was used and likelihood-ratio tests on penalised log-likelihoods are
approximate; the Poisson/NB count model fits cleanly (dispersion 1.12) but is driven by the 515/632
zero-gardening plots and adds nothing beyond the frequency axis; and habitat is partly defined by the
same land cover we regress on (mean 50 m urban cover 0.689 in Urban vs 0.006 in Scrublands), which
makes the Urban row context-confirming rather than independent evidence. Species identity per plot is
unavailable, so we cannot separate "gardening species are poor colonisers" from "gardening records
concentrate where ornamentals are planted" — though the species table corroborates the former weakly
(mean habitat.range 1.95 for Gardening vs 2.77 for Unintentional; one-sided Mann-Whitney p = 0.042,
weakening to p = 0.082 when the 4 duplicated rows are dropped).

**One-sentence version:** in the low-urban-cover, low-elevation part of the landscape the gardening
pathway switches prevalence style across habitat types — *frequent and co-dominant* (present in ~45-50%
of plots and >50% of the invader pool) in moist disturbed Wetlands and Coastal plots versus *frequent
but subordinate* (equally often encountered, only 19-30% of the invader pool, outnumbered by
unintentional introductions) in Urban and Croplands plots — and this habitat-driven switch is visible
only outside the urbanisation saturation zone (<20% urban cover), because heavy urban introduction
pressure homogenises habitat pathways; in the dry upland habitats (Rock outcrops, Scrublands, forests,
Meadows) gardening is too rarely recorded for any style to be determined.
